## 3. The Session Limit and the Conference Limit

3.1 **Two pools for all your Instances.** Your Session Limit is one pool of AI
Sessions, and your Conference Limit one pool of Conferences, each shared by every
Instance, replica and site that runs under your License Key. The two pools are
independent and both apply: a Conference uses one Conference however many
participants it has and whatever they use, and each participant in it that uses an AI
Capability also uses one AI Session. On the Free Plan, for example, one caller
talking to an AI agent, where the caller's participant uses speech-to-text and
text-to-speech, uses 1 of 10 Conferences and 1 of 4 AI Sessions; a three-way
Conference in which only one participant uses AI Capabilities uses 1 Conference and 1
AI Session. Capacity follows your traffic to wherever it arrives, including on
failover (Section 3.5), and you don't have to divide it between Instances yourself.
Every other License Key Synauson issues to you, including one for End User
installations (Section 4.4) and each Developer License (Section 2.8), has its own
pools, Session Limit and Conference Limit, unless your Order Form groups them into
one.

3.2 **Burst allowance.** On a paid Plan, when a pool is full, the Software admits
further AI Sessions up to 25% of the AI Sessions you bought, and further Conferences
up to 25% of the Conferences you bought, in each case rounded up to a whole AI
Session or Conference, or up to any higher allowance, at most 200% of what you
bought, that your Order Form states. With 10
AI Sessions bought, for example, your AI Session pool is 14 and you can run up to 17;
with 10 Conferences bought, your Conference pool is 20 and you can run up to 23. The
Free Plan's base of 4 AI Sessions and 10 Conferences never has a burst, and neither
does a Developer License. Each burst belongs to its whole pool and is shared across
your Instances: each Instance's share (Section 3.5) carries part of it, and a single
Instance gets all of it. Using the burst is never a breach of this Agreement, and the
busiest 5% of the hours in each month (about 36 hours) are set aside when Section 3.6
measures your use.

3.3 **No dropped calls.** Neither your Session Limit nor your Conference Limit ever
ends a Conference, an AI Session or a Call in progress. Once a burst allowance is
used up, new AI Sessions or new Conferences, as the case may be, are refused until
room frees up. A full Conference pool refuses only new Conferences, never a Call
joining a Conference already running. A Call that uses no AI Capability needs no AI
Session, and refusing a Call an AI Session does not end its Conference.

3.4 **Usage Reports.** To share the pools, each Instance on every Plan sends the
Synauson Services a Usage Report about every 30 seconds, one more when its AI
Sessions or its Conferences near its share of that pool, and a last one when it
shuts down cleanly. Section 12.3(c) lists everything a Usage Report contains. Each
report carries the Instance's peak numbers of AI Sessions and of Conferences for
every minute the Synauson Services have not yet received,
up to a day of minutes per report; the rest follow in later reports. After an
interruption, the Instance therefore uploads the minutes it could not send earlier,
under the identifier in use when they were counted. It does so after a restart too
when it keeps a state directory as the Documentation describes. A Usage Report never
contains Your Content (such as audio or transcripts), conference or participant
identifiers, or phone numbers or other caller identifiers. The Software writes each
Usage Report it sends for its own process to its log at debug level, so you can see
what is sent. Usage Reports are part of licensing. They are separate from the Model
Improvement Program, and joining or leaving the Program does not change them.

3.5 **Failover and offline Instances.** While connected, each Instance holds a share
of each of your pools as a lease of about 10 minutes, renewed with each Usage Report,
and the shares of a limited pool add up to that pool. If an Instance stops reporting,
its shares stay reserved for it until its lease ends, because it may still be
admitting AI Sessions and Conferences; then the
shares pass to the Instances still reporting, so the capacity follows your traffic.
This applies to an Instance that stops without a clean shutdown too, so an Instance
restarted within those 10 minutes may have less capacity until the old lease ends. An
Instance that cannot reach the Synauson Services keeps running for the period Section
9.4 allows and keeps the capacity it had: it admits AI Sessions and Conferences up to
the shares it last held and the burst on those shares (Section 3.2). It remembers
those shares across restarts when it keeps a state directory, and uses each only
while the limit of its pool is unchanged; Section 9.7 covers expiry while offline. An
Instance that has a License File but has never received a share of a pool admits
nothing from that pool until it connects: without a share of the AI Session pool it
admits no AI Sessions, and without a share of the Conference pool it starts no
Conferences, with or without AI Capabilities, unless your Conference Limit is
unlimited. The Instance keeps its per-minute
counts and sends them when the connection returns. Because each Instance admits AI
Sessions and Conferences against its own shares, an Instance may briefly refuse a new
AI Session or Conference while another holds unused room, until its next Usage Report
rebalances the shares.

3.6 **Sustained use above the pool.** Use above your Session Limit or your Conference
Limit is never a breach of this Agreement. Synauson measures each pool separately.
For each License Key, each pool and each hour, Synauson takes the highest number of
AI Sessions, or of Conferences, running at the same time across the Instances under
that key, as their Usage Reports show. Any hour in which an Instance used a share
after its lease had ended (Section 3.5), and so may have overlapped with the
Instances that took that share over, is counted as zero, and so, for Conferences, is
any hour in which an Instance under that key ran a version of the Software whose
Usage Reports do not count Conferences. Your use of a pool for a calendar month (UTC)
is the 95th percentile of its hourly figures: every hour of the month counts, an hour
with no Usage Report also counts as zero, and after the busiest 5% of the month's
hours are set aside, your use is the highest hourly figure that remains. The notice,
invoicing and three-month rules below apply to each pool on its own. If that measure
is above that key's Session Limit or Conference Limit, as the case may be, Synauson
will tell you, with the figures. The excess is that measure minus that limit. For
each later month in which it recurs, Synauson may invoice the excess, showing that
month's figures, at your Plan's per-session or per-conference rate prorated for one
month, as your Order Form states or, if you have no Order Form or it states no rate,
as the price list on Synauson's website states. Synauson will not invoice excess for
the month in which it tells you or any earlier month, and after three months in a row
without excess it must tell you again before invoicing. You may move to a larger
Session Limit or Conference Limit at any time instead. Excess on the Free Plan or
under a Developer License is never invoiced.

